  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$",
  "worked_solution": "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{\u2032}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{\u2032}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{\u2032}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
